
Prefix CD
Debug 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Codec-level decisions: the numbers that pick a representation rather
# than code a pixel.  Almost none of them appear on the wire, but they
# decide what does, so moving one moves the output.  The two that *are*
# wire format are frozen below and say so.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# The cross-plane blend weight scale.  weight_shift and weight_scale are
# one number in two forms -- the consumer derives the scale from the shift
# so they cannot drift -- and weight_round/weight_round3 are the rounding
# offsets applied before the shift.
Const 0

Number weight_shift,     1,0!111
Number weight_round,     1,0!101000
Number weight_round3,    1,0!1000000

# weight_bits and weight_bias are the wire field the fitted weights travel
# in: code_plane_descs writes weight+bias as a weight_bits-wide field and
# the decoder reads the same width back.  That makes them format, not
# tuning.  A wider field only wastes header bits; a narrower one truncates
# the weight *and* shortens the header, so the decoder reads the next field
# from the wrong offset and the bitstream desynchronises -- weight_bits 8
# -> 0 corrupts every multi-plane image and makes x_ep fail outright.
# There is no compression gradient here for an optimizer to climb, so the
# declaration takes them out of the search space rather than relying on a
# clamp at the point of use.
#
# Const 1 rather than a leading "-": "-" toggles Const, so a "-" line in a
# Const 0 section goes *live* under ./mk.sh release, which is the opposite
# of frozen (IDX-NOTES.md sec.5).
Const 1

Number weight_bits,      1,0!1000
Number weight_bias,      1,0!1000000

Const 0

# The least-squares fit's ridge term, as the denominator of a unit
# fraction.  A ridge of 0.1 is not a dyadic rational, so it cannot go in
# as an exact mantissa the way the NLMS constants do; 1.0/10.0 in double
# is bit-for-bit the double the literal 0.1 denotes, which a fixed-point
# approximation would not be.

Number fit_ridge_den,    1,0!1010

# The DC window search: how wide a window it starts from on each of the
# two histograms, and the sentinel each stops at.

Number dc_window_lo,     1,0!100
Number dc_window_hi,     1,0!10000000000
Number dc_diff_lo,       1,0!0
Number dc_diff_hi,       1,0!1000000000

# Decision margins.  A representation that is only marginally better is not
# worth the risk of a different code path, so several trials keep the incumbent
# unless the challenger wins by more than a fraction of its cost.  Each is the
# shift in "beats it by more than 1/2^shift".

Number keep_shift,     1,0!1100
Number model_shift,    1,0!101

# Strip splitting: the trial is only run when the two halves of a plane differ
# enough in alphabet size to plausibly want different coding modes -- the height
# below which a split is pointless, the alphabet size that counts as small, the
# ratio and the offset in the "much larger" test, and the shortest strip worth
# coding.

Number strip_min_h,    1,0!100000
Number strip_small,    1,0!10000
Number strip_ratio,    1,0!10
Number strip_offset,   1,0!1000
Number strip_min_rows, 1,0!1000
